  "context_pill": "Scientific Research Deductions",
  "example_summary": "A Ltd. calculates its Section 35 deductions for P.Y. 2025-26, covering external contributions to IISc and X Ltd., specific IIT projects, and in-house R&D while excluding land costs.",
  "source_provision": "### Master Statutory Summary Matrix: Section 35 Scientific Research Deductions\n\n| Nature of Expenditure / Contribution | Statutory Provision | Eligible Assessee | Rate of Deduction | Key Statutory Conditions & Exclusions |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Sum paid to notified approved Research Association / University / College | Section 35(1)(ii) | Any assessee | 100% | Must be used for scientific research approved by the prescribed authority. |\n| Sum paid to a Company registered in India for scientific research | Section 35(1)(iia) | Any assessee | 100% | Company's main object must be scientific research and development approved by prescribed authority. |\n| Sum paid to National Laboratory / University / IIT for approved programme | Section 35(2AA) | Any assessee | 100% | Specific direction and approval for scientific research programme required. |\n| In-house Revenue & Capital Expenditure on Research & Development | Section 35(2AB) | Company engaged in biotechnology or manufacturing | 100% | **Exclusion:** Cost of acquisition of land is strictly **not eligible** for deduction under capital expenditure. |\n\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Statutory Trap on Land Cost: Under Section 35(2AB), while capital expenditure on in-house research facilities qualifies for deduction, any expenditure incurred on the acquisition of land is expressly excluded from the eligible capital base.\n\n\n\n> [!NOTE] Approval Requirements: All contributions made to external institutions under Section 35(1) and Section 35(2AA) mandate prior notification and approval from the prescribed scientific authority to claim statutory deductions.",
